= Solution
{parent=a}

In <spherical symmetry>, the velocity is radial and constant over each sphere. Hence
$$
Q(R,t)=4\pi R^2u(R,t).
$$
Apply the <divergence theorem> to the fluid shell between radii $R_1$ and $R_2$. Since the flow is <incompressible flow>[incompressible],
$$
0=\int_{\text{shell}}\nabla\cdot u\,dV
=Q(R_2,t)-Q(R_1,t).
$$
Thus $Q$ is independent of $R$ and is a function of time alone. This is the flux law for <spherically symmetric incompressible radial flow>.

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

= b
{parent=16a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The radial velocity and <velocity potential> are
$$
u(r,t)=\frac{Q(t)}{4\pi r^2},
\qquad
\phi(r,t)=-\frac{Q(t)}{4\pi r}.
$$
Choose the gauge in the <unsteady Bernoulli equation> by evaluating it at infinity, where $u=0$, $\phi=0$, and $p=p_0$:
$$
\phi_t+\frac12u^2+\frac p\rho=\frac{p_0}\rho.
$$
At the cavity surface the vacuum pressure is zero, and the <kinematic boundary condition> gives $u(a,t)=\dot a$. Since
$$
\phi_t(a,t)=-\frac1{4\pi a}\frac{dQ}{dt},
$$
Bernoulli's equation becomes
$$
-\frac1{4\pi a}\frac{dQ}{dt}
+\frac{\dot a^2}{2}
=\frac{p_0}{\rho}.
$$
Therefore
$$
\boxed{
\frac1{4\pi a}\frac{dQ}{dt}
-\frac{\dot a^2}{2}
=-\frac{p_0}{\rho}
}.
$$

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

= c
{parent=16a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

At the moving boundary,
$$
Q=4\pi a^2\dot a.
$$
Substitution into the result of part (b) gives the <Rayleigh collapse of a spherical cavity>[Rayleigh collapse equation]
$$
a\ddot a+\frac32\dot a^2=-\frac{p_0}{\rho}.
$$
Treat $v=\dot a$ as a function of $a$, so that $\ddot a=v\,dv/da$. With $w=v^2$, the equation becomes
$$
a\frac{dw}{da}+3w=-\frac{2p_0}{\rho}.
$$
Multiplication by the <integrating factor> $a^3$ after division by $a$ gives
$$
\frac d{da}(a^3w)=-\frac{2p_0}{\rho}a^2.
$$
Using $w(a_0)=0$,
$$
w(a)=\frac{2p_0}{3\rho}
\left(\frac{a_0^3}{a^3}-1\right).
$$
The collapsing branch has negative radial velocity, so
$$
\boxed{
\dot a=-\sqrt{\frac{2p_0}{3\rho}
\left(\frac{a_0^3}{a^3}-1\right)}
}.
$$

