= Solution
{parent=a}

Here $X$ has the <binomial distribution> $\operatorname{Bin}(n,\theta)$, so, up to a factor independent of $\theta$, the <likelihood function> is
$$
L(\theta;X)=\theta^X(1-\theta)^{n-X}.
$$
For $0<X<n$, differentiating the <log-likelihood> gives
$$
\frac X\theta-\frac{n-X}{1-\theta}=0,
$$
and the boundary cases give the same formula. Thus the <binomial proportion maximum-likelihood estimator> is
$$
\boxed{\widehat\theta=\frac Xn}.
$$

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

= b
{parent=17h}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The estimator is unbiased because $\mathbb E_\theta X=n\theta$, and
$$
\operatorname{Var}_\theta(\widehat\theta)
=\frac1{n^2}\operatorname{Var}_\theta(X)
=\frac{\theta(1-\theta)}n.
$$
The <bias-variance decomposition of mean squared error> therefore gives
$$
\boxed{f(\theta)
=\mathbb E_\theta[(\widehat\theta-\theta)^2]
=\frac{\theta(1-\theta)}n}.
$$
The quadratic $\theta(1-\theta)$ is maximized at $\theta=1/2$, so
$$
\boxed{\sup_{0<\theta<1}f(\theta)=\frac1{4n}}.
$$

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

= c
{parent=17h}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The uniform prior is the <Beta distribution> $\operatorname{Beta}(1,1)$. By <Beta-binomial conjugacy>, after observing $X$ heads the posterior is
$$
\theta\mid X\sim\operatorname{Beta}(X+1,n-X+1).
$$
The <Bayes estimator under squared error loss> is the posterior mean, hence
$$
\boxed{\widehat\theta_B=\frac{X+1}{n+2}}.
$$

Solved by gpt-5.6-sol high.

= d
{parent=17h}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

For the weighted loss, the <Bayes estimator under parameter-weighted squared error> is the mean of the posterior after multiplication by
$$
\theta^{\alpha-1}(1-\theta)^{\beta-1}.
$$
Since the original posterior density is proportional to
$$
\theta^X(1-\theta)^{n-X},
$$
the reweighted density is $\operatorname{Beta}(X+\alpha,n-X+\beta)$. Its mean gives
$$
\widetilde\theta=\frac{X+\alpha}{n+\alpha+\beta}.
$$
Writing this in the required form,
$$
\boxed{
\widetilde\theta
=w\widehat\theta+(1-w)\theta_0,
\qquad
w=\frac n{n+\alpha+\beta},
\qquad
\theta_0=\frac{\alpha}{\alpha+\beta}
}.
$$

